WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.620
&gt;&gt; Good morning everyone.

00:00:06.626 --> 00:00:08.640
So, it's my very great
pleasure to introduce

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.520
Kartik Nayak from
the University of Maryland.

00:00:10.521 --> 00:00:12.910
At the moment, he's
a fifth year PhD student

00:00:12.911 --> 00:00:15.205
under Prof. Katz and Prof. Shi.

00:00:15.206 --> 00:00:17.610
And he's been a recipient

00:00:17.611 --> 00:00:19.560
of a Google Fellowship
in Security,

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:22.050
and he'll be talking to us
today Oblivious Computation.

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:26.410
&gt;&gt; All right.
Good morning everyone.

00:00:26.411 --> 00:00:27.780
I'm very excited to be here.

00:00:27.781 --> 00:00:29.400
Today, I'll be talking
about how to use

00:00:29.401 --> 00:00:31.010
oblivious computation
to compute in

00:00:31.011 --> 00:00:32.885
private data and to
obfuscate programs.

00:00:32.886 --> 00:00:34.990
And towards the end, I'll
talk a little bit more about

00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:36.200
my research in general and some

00:00:36.201 --> 00:00:38.370
of my ongoing and future work.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:41.040
So, it is if you have
the scenario where we

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:42.930
have two or more
hospitals, and together,

00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:44.820
they're trying to compute
information of that type,

00:00:44.821 --> 00:00:47.230
is some treatment better
than some other treatment?

00:00:47.231 --> 00:00:49.530
And they want to
do this based on

00:00:49.531 --> 00:00:51.090
the joint information of all

00:00:51.091 --> 00:00:52.970
the hospitals
patient data put together.

00:00:52.971 --> 00:00:55.285
But laws, such as
HIPAA, do not allow

00:00:55.286 --> 00:00:58.475
one hospital to share its data
with another hospital.

00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:00.755
So, you have the scenario where

00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:02.270
you have your input data which

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:03.730
is patient data that is private,

00:01:03.731 --> 00:01:05.185
and yet you're trying to

00:01:05.186 --> 00:01:07.140
perform some computation
on top of this,

00:01:07.141 --> 00:01:09.865
and the computation
itself may be public.

00:01:09.866 --> 00:01:13.670
You have a complementary
scenario where you have

00:01:13.671 --> 00:01:15.080
companies such as 23andMe which

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:16.965
have patented
genomic algorithms.

00:01:16.966 --> 00:01:20.585
And they want to send
their programs to the end user,

00:01:20.586 --> 00:01:22.460
in such a way that
the end user can

00:01:22.461 --> 00:01:25.320
find its own
ancestry information.

00:01:25.321 --> 00:01:28.350
In this scenario, 23andMe

00:01:28.351 --> 00:01:30.135
wants end users to
use their program,

00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:32.110
yet not learn what
the program is doing

00:01:32.111 --> 00:01:33.420
because the program forms

00:01:33.421 --> 00:01:35.620
the trade secret
for such companies.

00:01:35.621 --> 00:01:37.690
So, you have a dual scenario.

00:01:37.691 --> 00:01:39.335
You're aware
a program is private,

00:01:39.336 --> 00:01:43.145
but the user input themselves
may or may not be public.

00:01:43.146 --> 00:01:46.150
So, my research has
focused on designing and

00:01:46.151 --> 00:01:48.420
building systems for such
privacy-preserving computation.

00:01:48.421 --> 00:01:49.830
And as I just mentioned,

00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:51.820
broadly it falls
into two categories.

00:01:51.821 --> 00:01:54.610
One, how to secretly

00:01:54.611 --> 00:01:57.510
outsource data and
enable computation on it.

00:01:57.511 --> 00:01:59.955
And second, how to
obfuscate programs.

00:01:59.956 --> 00:02:01.860
And today, I'm going
to discuss this with

00:02:01.861 --> 00:02:04.375
the lens of access-pattern
Obliviousness,

00:02:04.376 --> 00:02:05.710
which in a nutshell is

00:02:05.711 --> 00:02:08.385
the art of hiding
memory access patterns.

00:02:08.386 --> 00:02:10.360
And to start today,
I'm going to first

00:02:10.361 --> 00:02:12.545
discuss access-pattern
Obliviousness,

00:02:12.546 --> 00:02:13.830
and this is going to be

00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:15.915
a short introduction
to what it is.

00:02:15.916 --> 00:02:18.255
And then I'll move on
to contributions on

00:02:18.256 --> 00:02:20.035
computation on private data

00:02:20.036 --> 00:02:21.775
and finally,
obfuscation of programs.

00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:23.990
And towards the end, I'll
talk a little bit more about

00:02:23.991 --> 00:02:25.150
my other research on

00:02:25.151 --> 00:02:28.610
block chains and some of
my ongoing and future work.

00:02:28.611 --> 00:02:31.600
So, whenever some computation
is being performed,

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:32.900
you have a CPU and memory,

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:37.320
where your memory will store
your data and your program,

00:02:37.321 --> 00:02:39.630
and whenever an instruction is

00:02:39.631 --> 00:02:41.915
being executed on
the address and database,

00:02:41.916 --> 00:02:44.610
you will have the
corresponding memory location

00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:47.710
that is being accessed
and the data would.

00:02:47.711 --> 00:02:50.665
If I were to enable
computation in private data,

00:02:50.666 --> 00:02:54.020
the first step would be
to keep this data private.

00:02:54.021 --> 00:02:55.550
And one way to achieve

00:02:55.551 --> 00:02:57.815
this is to leverage
a secure processor,

00:02:57.816 --> 00:03:00.475
which ensures that
all the data in your memory is

00:03:00.476 --> 00:03:04.025
encrypted and the data on
the database is also encrypted.

00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:05.950
But what it still do revealed

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:09.160
is which memory location
you're accessing.

00:03:09.161 --> 00:03:13.100
Such secure processors exists
in the form of Intel SGX.

00:03:13.101 --> 00:03:14.820
But the question is,
you're still revealing

00:03:14.821 --> 00:03:18.690
these addresses and whether
it is okay to do that.

00:03:18.691 --> 00:03:22.030
If you look at this
specific example which says,

00:03:22.031 --> 00:03:23.670
if some secret variable is true,

00:03:23.671 --> 00:03:25.745
then I'm going to access
memory location X,

00:03:25.746 --> 00:03:27.805
otherwise I'm going to
access memory location Y.

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:29.400
And just based on which memory

00:03:29.401 --> 00:03:30.900
location is being accessed,

00:03:30.901 --> 00:03:34.100
now you would know exactly what
the secret variable is.

00:03:34.101 --> 00:03:36.225
And this tells you that,

00:03:36.226 --> 00:03:38.160
well, access patterns can

00:03:38.161 --> 00:03:40.770
indeed reveal
some secret information.

00:03:40.771 --> 00:03:43.490
And if this seems
like a toy example,

00:03:43.491 --> 00:03:45.810
there exist model level attacks,

00:03:45.811 --> 00:03:48.990
actually this attack is
right from the MassArt lab

00:03:48.991 --> 00:03:52.670
where some competition
was being performed on

00:03:52.671 --> 00:03:56.665
JPEG images and this was
done using Intel SGX.

00:03:56.666 --> 00:03:58.310
And in the process, just by

00:03:58.311 --> 00:03:59.970
looking at base level
access patterns,

00:03:59.971 --> 00:04:01.830
one could recover the image

00:04:01.831 --> 00:04:04.125
to the right from
the image to the left.

00:04:04.126 --> 00:04:06.630
And this tells you
access patterns can

00:04:06.631 --> 00:04:09.445
indeed leak a lot of
information about your data.

00:04:09.446 --> 00:04:12.465
And all of these attacks
are a little of the recent.

00:04:12.466 --> 00:04:14.280
The problem of protecting

00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:16.120
access patterns was discussed on

00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:17.770
three decades ago
when Bullrich and

00:04:17.771 --> 00:04:20.450
Ostrovsky came up with
the notion of an oblivious RAM,

00:04:20.451 --> 00:04:22.340
which is a
cryptographic primitive

00:04:22.341 --> 00:04:24.705
to provably hide
access patterns to data.

00:04:24.706 --> 00:04:27.570
In this talk today, it
is not necessary for you

00:04:27.571 --> 00:04:30.160
to understand an oblivious
and a schema as such,

00:04:30.161 --> 00:04:32.905
but just to give
you some intuition,

00:04:32.906 --> 00:04:35.465
here's what all
oblivious RAM schemes do.

00:04:35.466 --> 00:04:38.135
Let us say I want to access
memory location five,

00:04:38.136 --> 00:04:41.165
then an old RAM scheme would
access memory location five,

00:04:41.166 --> 00:04:44.000
but in addition, it would
make a couple more of

00:04:44.001 --> 00:04:45.430
a few more obfuscating accesses

00:04:45.431 --> 00:04:47.785
to the human location
seven and two.

00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:50.615
And once I perform this access,

00:04:50.616 --> 00:04:52.080
what most of what RAM schemes do

00:04:52.081 --> 00:04:53.830
is, they shuffle data around.

00:04:53.831 --> 00:04:56.595
The reason why you shuffle
this data is because,

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:58.070
if you do not shuffle it and

00:04:58.071 --> 00:04:59.520
if you want to
access five again,

00:04:59.521 --> 00:05:01.310
then you may reveal some sort of

00:05:01.311 --> 00:05:03.410
frequency or
co-location information.

00:05:03.411 --> 00:05:05.770
But if you shuffle
data around, perhaps,

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:07.295
if you're still
accessing five again,

00:05:07.296 --> 00:05:08.480
you would be able to hide which

00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:10.430
memory location
you're accessing.

00:05:10.431 --> 00:05:12.770
And because you are
making these obfuscating

00:05:12.771 --> 00:05:14.615
accesses and shuffling data,

00:05:14.616 --> 00:05:16.490
you actually end up touching

00:05:16.491 --> 00:05:17.540
more memory locations than you

00:05:17.541 --> 00:05:19.390
need and one key metric that

00:05:19.391 --> 00:05:21.590
you're trying to reduce in
an oblivious RAM scheme,

00:05:21.591 --> 00:05:23.250
is bandwidth blow
up or the number of

00:05:23.251 --> 00:05:25.280
memory locations that
is being accessed.

00:05:25.281 --> 00:05:27.200
And typically,
oblivious RAM schemes

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:29.830
have a point of
logarithm bandwidth blow up.

00:05:29.831 --> 00:05:32.570
So, given this background,

00:05:32.571 --> 00:05:34.195
one key challenge in this space

00:05:34.196 --> 00:05:36.300
is to make access
pattern Obliviousness

00:05:36.301 --> 00:05:39.090
efficient and this
we need to do given

00:05:39.091 --> 00:05:42.325
that we know that there is
a lower bound that says,

00:05:42.326 --> 00:05:45.440
if I have N passive memory
locations, by passive I mean,

00:05:45.441 --> 00:05:47.505
you can only read and
write to this memory,

00:05:47.506 --> 00:05:49.435
then you need to encode

00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:52.280
at least a logarithmic overhead
for every access.

00:05:52.281 --> 00:05:54.420
And the base of the logarithm
over here is the number of

00:05:54.421 --> 00:05:56.960
registers stored in the CPU.

00:05:56.961 --> 00:05:58.195
So, given this lower bound,

00:05:58.196 --> 00:06:00.450
one thing that you can do is you

00:06:00.451 --> 00:06:02.910
can design a scheme that
actually meets this lower bound

00:06:02.911 --> 00:06:06.000
with as good constants as
possible or try to circumvent

00:06:06.001 --> 00:06:07.490
this lower bound all together

00:06:07.491 --> 00:06:11.380
by changing the assumptions
involved in the lower bound.

00:06:11.381 --> 00:06:13.930
Second, whenever I have to

00:06:13.931 --> 00:06:16.760
move to performing
a large amount of competition,

00:06:16.761 --> 00:06:19.150
I would want to
parallel my computation,

00:06:19.151 --> 00:06:21.560
and turns out parallelism with

00:06:21.561 --> 00:06:24.950
Obliviousness comes up with
its own set of challenges.

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.645
For example, now the
communication pattern in between

00:06:27.646 --> 00:06:31.090
the processors should not
reveal any more information.

00:06:31.091 --> 00:06:32.970
And this constraint
is in addition to

00:06:32.971 --> 00:06:35.270
the standard constraints of
pattern programming where you

00:06:35.271 --> 00:06:37.240
would want to have
a low processing time

00:06:37.241 --> 00:06:40.350
and low communication
between processors.

00:06:40.351 --> 00:06:42.170
And finally, I would argue

00:06:42.171 --> 00:06:43.550
whenever you want
to design a system,

00:06:43.551 --> 00:06:45.315
you would want to design it with

00:06:45.316 --> 00:06:47.340
some practical
constraints in mind.

00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:49.990
For example, if I'm trying
to use a secure processor,

00:06:49.991 --> 00:06:51.330
I should know exactly what

00:06:51.331 --> 00:06:53.435
properties such a secure
processor it provides,

00:06:53.436 --> 00:06:57.630
and that is useful both for
security and efficiency.

00:06:57.631 --> 00:07:00.500
For instance, one question
that you may ask is can

00:07:00.501 --> 00:07:03.390
the trusted processes store
stated in between locations.

00:07:03.391 --> 00:07:06.560
Let us start to ask
this question to Intel SGX.

00:07:06.561 --> 00:07:10.245
So, whenever Intel SGX is
executing some instruction,

00:07:10.246 --> 00:07:11.480
it has context of

00:07:11.481 --> 00:07:13.320
the previous instruction
it has executed.

00:07:13.321 --> 00:07:15.070
So, in that sense,

00:07:15.071 --> 00:07:17.640
you can think of such a process
as being stateful.

00:07:17.641 --> 00:07:20.445
But more recently,
you have seen attacks,

00:07:20.446 --> 00:07:21.715
called as rollback attacks,

00:07:21.716 --> 00:07:24.500
where you could switch
off an Intel SGX machine

00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:25.910
and then provide it with

00:07:25.911 --> 00:07:28.715
an old state and then
rollback execution.

00:07:28.716 --> 00:07:30.790
So, in that sense,
you can think of

00:07:30.791 --> 00:07:33.295
Intel SGX as being
a stateless processor.

00:07:33.296 --> 00:07:35.195
And in the second part
of my talk,

00:07:35.196 --> 00:07:36.540
I'm going to discuss how

00:07:36.541 --> 00:07:38.950
such exceptions in
conjunction with

00:07:38.951 --> 00:07:43.120
Obliviousness can actually
matter in security of schemes.

00:07:43.121 --> 00:07:45.400
But more generally, I would say

00:07:45.401 --> 00:07:47.030
that if privacy preserving

00:07:47.031 --> 00:07:48.480
competition were
to be a success,

00:07:48.481 --> 00:07:50.570
it is essential for the
field of cryptography,

00:07:50.571 --> 00:07:52.560
architecture, systems,
and programming

00:07:52.561 --> 00:07:55.180
languages to work
in conjunction.

00:07:55.181 --> 00:07:58.125
With that, let me move on to

00:07:58.126 --> 00:08:00.010
discussing my contributions

00:08:00.011 --> 00:08:01.630
towards computation
in private data,

00:08:01.631 --> 00:08:03.955
and specifically,
I'm going to talk

00:08:03.956 --> 00:08:08.160
about a Secure Graph-parallel
Framework, called GraphSC.

00:08:08.161 --> 00:08:10.440
And in this framework,

00:08:10.441 --> 00:08:12.500
I'm going to show
how it is usable,

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:14.080
that is you can
design programs with

00:08:14.081 --> 00:08:16.080
programming abstractions
that you already know,

00:08:16.081 --> 00:08:17.720
in such a way that
this framework

00:08:17.721 --> 00:08:20.375
converts it into
an efficient parallel execution,

00:08:20.376 --> 00:08:21.860
and you achieve some notion of

00:08:21.861 --> 00:08:23.410
security that I'm
going to discuss.

00:08:23.411 --> 00:08:25.385
And you are going to
see results which say,

00:08:25.386 --> 00:08:27.490
what took us for previously

00:08:27.491 --> 00:08:31.640
can be performed now 20x faster.

00:08:31.641 --> 00:08:35.490
And specifically,
let us take this as

00:08:35.491 --> 00:08:37.965
a learning example where
let's say you have Netflix,

00:08:37.966 --> 00:08:39.370
and Netflix wants to

00:08:39.371 --> 00:08:41.865
compute user recommendations
on this data set.

00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:43.665
So, this data set has

00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:45.525
in the first column
you can see users,

00:08:45.526 --> 00:08:46.830
in the second column you can see

00:08:46.831 --> 00:08:49.200
movies and in
the third column you can

00:08:49.201 --> 00:08:53.515
see what rating each user
has given to the movie.

00:08:53.516 --> 00:08:55.410
And given this Netflix wants

00:08:55.411 --> 00:08:56.890
to compute user recommendations,

00:08:56.891 --> 00:08:58.755
that is for a given user,

00:08:58.756 --> 00:09:01.945
which movie the user
may want to see next.

00:09:01.946 --> 00:09:04.535
And even if we don't
need security over here,

00:09:04.536 --> 00:09:06.695
one thing that Netflix
would want to do is,

00:09:06.696 --> 00:09:08.720
given such datasets
tend to be large,

00:09:08.721 --> 00:09:11.315
you may want to
exploit parallelism.

00:09:11.316 --> 00:09:13.860
And just for explosiveness,

00:09:13.861 --> 00:09:16.420
you may want to use
frameworks such as GraphLab.

00:09:16.421 --> 00:09:21.470
And GraphLab is just one
such framework in a class of

00:09:21.471 --> 00:09:24.200
frameworks which is
targeted for a class of

00:09:24.201 --> 00:09:25.300
algorithms called Graph-parallel

00:09:25.301 --> 00:09:28.030
Algorithms or
Vertex-centric programs.

00:09:28.031 --> 00:09:29.960
The reason why they're
called Vertex-centric

00:09:29.961 --> 00:09:31.040
programs is because

00:09:31.041 --> 00:09:32.485
at any point in time you're

00:09:32.486 --> 00:09:34.070
always thinking like a Vertex.

00:09:34.071 --> 00:09:37.060
So, a Vertex in
the graph based on

00:09:37.061 --> 00:09:38.655
its own data and data
for its neighbors

00:09:38.656 --> 00:09:40.905
is trying to perform
some competition.

00:09:40.906 --> 00:09:43.900
For example, you can
think of PageRank,

00:09:43.901 --> 00:09:46.350
where the PageRank from
that page depends on

00:09:46.351 --> 00:09:47.790
its own PageRank and the

00:09:47.791 --> 00:09:50.910
PageRanks of other pages
that are pointing to it.

00:09:50.911 --> 00:09:53.600
In fact, the example
that I was describing

00:09:53.601 --> 00:09:55.470
earlier on computing
user recommendations,

00:09:55.471 --> 00:09:57.535
can be expressed as
a bipartite graph

00:09:57.536 --> 00:10:00.425
between users at one side
and movies at the other,

00:10:00.426 --> 00:10:01.700
and it just represent

00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:05.185
the rating that a user
has given to a movie.

00:10:05.186 --> 00:10:07.650
In terms of the result

00:10:07.651 --> 00:10:08.720
that I'm going to
discuss over here,

00:10:08.721 --> 00:10:09.760
in general applies to all my

00:10:09.761 --> 00:10:12.505
marketing operations as well.

00:10:12.506 --> 00:10:15.385
So, one of the reasons why

00:10:15.386 --> 00:10:18.170
these Graph-parallel
frameworks are nice is

00:10:18.171 --> 00:10:20.050
because you just
need to remember

00:10:20.051 --> 00:10:22.370
three simple abstractions
and they are called scatter,

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:25.415
gather and apply, and I'm
going to explain them in turn.

00:10:25.416 --> 00:10:27.930
And remember that at
each point in time,

00:10:27.931 --> 00:10:30.010
you need to think
of the competition

00:10:30.011 --> 00:10:31.765
as being from
a vertex's point of view.

00:10:31.766 --> 00:10:34.245
What would a vertex do next?

00:10:34.246 --> 00:10:37.400
The first obstruction is
a scattered obstruction,

00:10:37.401 --> 00:10:39.390
where every vertex
sends some data to

00:10:39.391 --> 00:10:41.100
its neighboring edges and

00:10:41.101 --> 00:10:44.130
the neighboring edges
update their own data.

00:10:44.131 --> 00:10:47.140
The second abstraction
is complementary,

00:10:47.141 --> 00:10:49.370
where overtakes aggregates data

00:10:49.371 --> 00:10:50.850
from all of
its neighboring edges,

00:10:50.851 --> 00:10:52.650
and the vertex may
update its own data.

00:10:52.651 --> 00:10:56.435
So the example over here
shows are gathered vertex B.

00:10:56.436 --> 00:11:00.255
And finally in
an apply operation,

00:11:00.256 --> 00:11:01.985
every vertex and every edge

00:11:01.986 --> 00:11:04.640
applies some function
to its own data.

00:11:04.641 --> 00:11:07.230
So the example over here shows

00:11:07.231 --> 00:11:09.360
an apply by all the vertices.

00:11:09.361 --> 00:11:11.600
And in terms of just having

00:11:11.601 --> 00:11:13.450
these three abstractions
is very powerful for

00:11:13.451 --> 00:11:16.290
a large class of algorithms.

00:11:16.291 --> 00:11:18.900
And the reason why these
Graph-parallel frameworks are

00:11:18.901 --> 00:11:21.610
nice is because
you have usability,

00:11:21.611 --> 00:11:22.810
you just need to express

00:11:22.811 --> 00:11:24.760
functions using
these three abstractions,

00:11:24.761 --> 00:11:26.470
and then these
Graph-parallel frameworks

00:11:26.471 --> 00:11:27.985
give you efficiency and

00:11:27.986 --> 00:11:29.820
parallelism that you can easily

00:11:29.821 --> 00:11:32.990
use the computers
that you already have.

00:11:32.991 --> 00:11:35.210
Given this, the goal of

00:11:35.211 --> 00:11:39.110
GraphSC was to have a secure
Graph-parallel framework,

00:11:39.111 --> 00:11:40.785
that is to have security

00:11:40.786 --> 00:11:42.795
in addition to all
of these three goods,

00:11:42.796 --> 00:11:45.685
and GraphSC gives
you exactly that.

00:11:45.686 --> 00:11:51.175
So let us see what I mean
by security in this context.

00:11:51.176 --> 00:11:54.420
So let us see say we have
a secured processor and

00:11:54.421 --> 00:11:57.190
it has your graph
store encrypted,

00:11:57.191 --> 00:12:00.040
and it is trying to
perform some computation,

00:12:00.041 --> 00:12:02.465
let's say computing
user recommendations.

00:12:02.466 --> 00:12:04.140
What I want in the end

00:12:04.141 --> 00:12:06.275
is to ensure that
this computation,

00:12:06.276 --> 00:12:10.330
it hides the data on
vertices and edges,

00:12:10.331 --> 00:12:13.210
and second, it hides
the structure of the graph.

00:12:13.211 --> 00:12:15.220
That is, the fact that there is

00:12:15.221 --> 00:12:17.990
an engine between two vertices
should not be revealed.

00:12:17.991 --> 00:12:20.230
And although I'm
explaining all of

00:12:20.231 --> 00:12:22.330
this assuming a secured
processor over here,

00:12:22.331 --> 00:12:24.960
later I'm going to relax
that by abstracting

00:12:24.961 --> 00:12:28.540
the secured processor using
two different parties,

00:12:28.541 --> 00:12:31.160
and running a second
computation protocol on it.

00:12:31.161 --> 00:12:32.700
But for now let us assume

00:12:32.701 --> 00:12:34.460
that we have access to
a secured processor.

00:12:34.461 --> 00:12:38.240
&gt;&gt; How is the data
encrypted? So how

00:12:38.241 --> 00:12:40.500
is this data gathered in
the first place and encrypted?

00:12:40.501 --> 00:12:43.600
&gt;&gt; So, in this particular
example you can assume that

00:12:43.601 --> 00:12:45.010
a user just submits

00:12:45.011 --> 00:12:46.330
an encrypted data and it just

00:12:46.331 --> 00:12:48.695
communicates with
a secured processor.

00:12:48.696 --> 00:12:51.120
In the second computation
scenario it's going

00:12:51.121 --> 00:12:52.530
to be used slightly different
way, or use a secret,

00:12:52.531 --> 00:12:56.675
shares its data to non-colluding
cloud service providers.

00:12:56.676 --> 00:12:59.290
So, this is the scenario if

00:12:59.291 --> 00:13:01.120
we have access to
a single processor.

00:13:01.121 --> 00:13:04.185
When we move to
multiple processors,

00:13:04.186 --> 00:13:06.950
then we have an additional
challenge in terms

00:13:06.951 --> 00:13:08.020
of how to split

00:13:08.021 --> 00:13:10.145
this computation into
multiple processors,

00:13:10.146 --> 00:13:13.980
and we still need to
ensure the same properties,

00:13:13.981 --> 00:13:16.930
that is we want to hide
data on vertices and edges,

00:13:16.931 --> 00:13:18.630
and hide the graph
structure, and

00:13:18.631 --> 00:13:20.300
turns out how you parallelize it

00:13:20.301 --> 00:13:24.560
may have an effect on how
we achieve these properties.

00:13:24.561 --> 00:13:27.650
So given this, let
us try to see how we

00:13:27.651 --> 00:13:31.445
can achieve this
in our scenario.

00:13:31.446 --> 00:13:35.455
Our first strawman would be
just to use an Oblivious RAM,

00:13:35.456 --> 00:13:37.200
and an Oblivious RAM

00:13:37.201 --> 00:13:39.955
ensures that all access
patterns are oblivious,

00:13:39.956 --> 00:13:41.660
and it is usable because I can

00:13:41.661 --> 00:13:44.045
use this with
any program that I like.

00:13:44.046 --> 00:13:46.280
But the problem
is that Oblivious

00:13:46.281 --> 00:13:48.550
RAM constructions
tend to be sequential,

00:13:48.551 --> 00:13:51.450
that is you cannot
easily parallelize it.

00:13:51.451 --> 00:13:54.850
And a much more subtle
constraint that comes into

00:13:54.851 --> 00:13:58.010
the picture is that once
your data is in an ORAM,

00:13:58.011 --> 00:13:59.775
all subsequent accessories will

00:13:59.776 --> 00:14:02.645
incur a poly-logarithmic word.

00:14:02.646 --> 00:14:05.630
So, given parallelism laws,

00:14:05.631 --> 00:14:08.200
something that Oblivious RAM
did not achieve,

00:14:08.201 --> 00:14:10.900
let us try to
achieve parallelism,

00:14:10.901 --> 00:14:15.420
and try to do something
weird for every vertex.

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:17.460
I have a secure processor

00:14:17.461 --> 00:14:20.760
trying to perform
the computation on that vertex.

00:14:20.761 --> 00:14:24.680
Such a solution will give
you parallelism easily.

00:14:24.681 --> 00:14:26.360
It gives you usability

00:14:26.361 --> 00:14:29.870
because you're still
in the GraphLab vault,

00:14:29.871 --> 00:14:32.430
and it also gives
you efficiency,

00:14:32.431 --> 00:14:35.275
but the problem is that this
does not give you security.

00:14:35.276 --> 00:14:36.970
And this is because the

00:14:36.971 --> 00:14:38.620
communication between
one processor and

00:14:38.621 --> 00:14:39.870
another processor will reveal

00:14:39.871 --> 00:14:42.680
information off
the existence of an edge.

00:14:42.681 --> 00:14:46.070
One way to fix this is
to ensure that there is

00:14:46.071 --> 00:14:48.925
an all to all communication
in between these processors,

00:14:48.926 --> 00:14:51.905
and that you will
achieve security,

00:14:51.906 --> 00:14:53.300
but now this would be at

00:14:53.301 --> 00:14:57.160
a cost of V squared
communication.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:01.030
One thing that
Prior Work has done,

00:15:01.031 --> 00:15:03.530
and this was for
this specific example,

00:15:03.531 --> 00:15:06.100
they came up with
a handcrafted computation that

00:15:06.101 --> 00:15:08.980
achieved some notion of
efficiency and it was secure.

00:15:08.981 --> 00:15:12.110
But the problem with
such approaches is that this

00:15:12.111 --> 00:15:15.175
was very specific
to one algorithm,

00:15:15.176 --> 00:15:17.580
and for every algorithm
you would have to

00:15:17.581 --> 00:15:21.610
think ground up on how to make
it parallel and Oblivious.

00:15:21.611 --> 00:15:24.010
And they only achieved

00:15:24.011 --> 00:15:27.205
some partial notion of
parallelism in this case.

00:15:27.206 --> 00:15:30.500
So given this, let us
see what our work does,

00:15:30.501 --> 00:15:32.240
and this time I'm
going to explain

00:15:32.241 --> 00:15:34.710
with the key idea involved
in an Oblivious Gather,

00:15:34.711 --> 00:15:36.645
and this I'm going
to first explain

00:15:36.646 --> 00:15:37.950
assuming a single processor,

00:15:37.951 --> 00:15:41.870
and then I'll move on to
how to parallelize this.

00:15:41.871 --> 00:15:45.035
And for this example
let us still

00:15:45.036 --> 00:15:46.790
assume that we have
this bipartite graph

00:15:46.791 --> 00:15:48.410
or let me slightly simplify

00:15:48.411 --> 00:15:51.025
this graph so that I
can fit it on the slide.

00:15:51.026 --> 00:15:54.050
And, you can still think of
the vertices to the left as

00:15:54.051 --> 00:15:55.170
being users and the vertices

00:15:55.171 --> 00:15:57.520
on the right as being movies.

00:15:57.521 --> 00:16:00.060
And let us say we have

00:16:00.061 --> 00:16:01.935
a very simple computation
that we are performing,

00:16:01.936 --> 00:16:04.965
and that is how many people
watch a particular movie.

00:16:04.966 --> 00:16:07.540
So on this vertex X in
the end you would want to

00:16:07.541 --> 00:16:11.220
see the data too being
stored over here.

00:16:11.221 --> 00:16:14.350
And because you are just
performing a counting operation,

00:16:14.351 --> 00:16:17.600
the data on each of
these edges is one.

00:16:17.601 --> 00:16:19.855
The way I'm going
to present my graph

00:16:19.856 --> 00:16:21.650
is using a simple array,

00:16:21.651 --> 00:16:23.865
where each cell of the array

00:16:23.866 --> 00:16:26.545
represents either a
vertex or an edge.

00:16:26.546 --> 00:16:28.160
For example, the first cell

00:16:28.161 --> 00:16:29.815
over here represents vertex C,

00:16:29.816 --> 00:16:33.230
the second cell represents
edge X and so on and so forth.

00:16:33.231 --> 00:16:36.160
And in general all vertices
are represented

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:39.780
by using a yellow background.

00:16:39.781 --> 00:16:43.750
The first step that I'm
going to do in this process

00:16:43.751 --> 00:16:46.620
is to sort this data using

00:16:46.621 --> 00:16:49.360
an Oblivious sort
operation in such a way

00:16:49.361 --> 00:16:53.210
that a vertex is preceded by
all of its incoming edges.

00:16:53.211 --> 00:16:55.625
So, you're going to see vertex X

00:16:55.626 --> 00:16:58.325
being preceded by
just A X and B X.

00:16:58.326 --> 00:17:00.635
An Oblivious sort operation

00:17:00.636 --> 00:17:02.990
by definition is data oblivious,

00:17:02.991 --> 00:17:06.515
so it does not reveal
any information about your data.

00:17:06.516 --> 00:17:10.295
And now, in a single
sequential scan,

00:17:10.296 --> 00:17:12.965
I can perform
my aggregation operation.

00:17:12.966 --> 00:17:15.240
So whenever I reach an edge, I'm

00:17:15.241 --> 00:17:16.900
going to collect data
and aggregate it,

00:17:16.901 --> 00:17:18.780
and whenever I reach a vertex,

00:17:18.781 --> 00:17:21.315
I'm going to deposit
that data there.

00:17:21.316 --> 00:17:24.690
So we collected one and
one from A X and B X,

00:17:24.691 --> 00:17:26.630
and deposited it at vertex X,

00:17:26.631 --> 00:17:28.860
and that is the simple
aggregation operation.

00:17:28.861 --> 00:17:30.490
So over here we are doing it

00:17:30.491 --> 00:17:32.180
for a simple counting operation,

00:17:32.181 --> 00:17:33.810
but in general you
can do this for

00:17:33.811 --> 00:17:37.400
any associative and
committed to operation.

00:17:37.401 --> 00:17:42.300
W resort operation in
principal takes E log V time,

00:17:42.301 --> 00:17:44.475
for a graph of E edges
and V vertices.

00:17:44.476 --> 00:17:47.090
&gt;&gt; So, in this computation
are you allowed

00:17:47.091 --> 00:17:50.920
to reveal the number
of vertices,

00:17:50.921 --> 00:17:53.905
and the number of vertices
on either side of the batch?

00:17:53.906 --> 00:17:56.080
&gt;&gt; No. So this computation by

00:17:56.081 --> 00:17:58.375
itself does not reveal
that information.

00:17:58.376 --> 00:18:01.385
So in practice what you will
end up doing over here...

00:18:01.386 --> 00:18:03.010
&gt;&gt; I'm asking where if you have

00:18:03.011 --> 00:18:04.810
a problem with computation,
you want to hide that.

00:18:04.811 --> 00:18:05.735
&gt;&gt; You want to hide that.

00:18:05.736 --> 00:18:07.745
So the computation by
itself does not reveal it,

00:18:07.746 --> 00:18:09.380
but what may happen is that

00:18:09.381 --> 00:18:10.985
your input and
output structure is

00:18:10.986 --> 00:18:12.560
such that the inputs and

00:18:12.561 --> 00:18:14.945
outputs itself reveal
how many vertices are there.

00:18:14.946 --> 00:18:17.690
&gt;&gt; Well, but in this case
I can learn the sum

00:18:17.691 --> 00:18:19.030
of vertices and edges based

00:18:19.031 --> 00:18:20.220
on your representation itself,

00:18:20.221 --> 00:18:22.385
right? The input there.

00:18:22.386 --> 00:18:25.770
&gt;&gt; No. So all of this data
is going to be encrypted,

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:28.455
so that you will not
know whether a vertex

00:18:28.456 --> 00:18:32.860
is a vertex or an edge. It's
a little more disarranged.

00:18:32.861 --> 00:18:34.150
&gt;&gt; I would know what the sum of.

00:18:34.151 --> 00:18:36.200
&gt;&gt; You would know
the summation of, yes...

00:18:36.201 --> 00:18:37.745
&gt;&gt; I would not know

00:18:37.746 --> 00:18:39.275
like what's the size of

00:18:39.276 --> 00:18:40.840
each partition is, or
how many edge is there.

00:18:40.841 --> 00:18:41.161
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:41.161 --> 00:18:42.830
&gt;&gt; And you want to
protect that even

00:18:42.831 --> 00:18:45.020
at the end of the computation?

00:18:45.021 --> 00:18:47.110
&gt;&gt; Yes, unless the initial input

00:18:47.111 --> 00:18:48.750
or the final output
reveals that.

00:18:48.751 --> 00:18:50.930
Because in many cases you'll
final output may just say,

00:18:50.931 --> 00:18:52.725
give me values of all vertices.

00:18:52.726 --> 00:18:54.160
&gt;&gt; Okay, thanks.

00:18:54.161 --> 00:18:57.040
&gt;&gt; So your Oblivious
sort operation over

00:18:57.041 --> 00:18:58.280
here takes E log V time for

00:18:58.281 --> 00:19:00.600
a graph of E edges
and V vertices,

00:19:00.601 --> 00:19:02.720
and in practice you
would actually end up

00:19:02.721 --> 00:19:05.960
using something
like a Bitonic sort

00:19:05.961 --> 00:19:08.245
which equals E log
squared V time,

00:19:08.246 --> 00:19:11.125
and the sequential scan
requires order E time,

00:19:11.126 --> 00:19:15.130
so this requires
a total work of E log V.

00:19:15.131 --> 00:19:18.750
One key aspect about
such an operation is that this

00:19:18.751 --> 00:19:20.155
can be easily parallelized

00:19:20.156 --> 00:19:21.945
when you have
multiple processors.

00:19:21.946 --> 00:19:24.340
So let us see how we do that.

00:19:24.341 --> 00:19:26.995
Remember the end of
an Oblivious sort?

00:19:26.996 --> 00:19:30.315
We have a vertex preceded by
all of its incoming edges,

00:19:30.316 --> 00:19:32.985
and in general this problem
looks like the following.

00:19:32.986 --> 00:19:35.660
You have an array of indisposed
white and yellow cells.

00:19:35.661 --> 00:19:37.305
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:19:37.306 --> 00:19:39.765
Would you be able to
correct that little thing?

00:19:39.766 --> 00:19:42.530
So what is output
of this computation?

00:19:42.531 --> 00:19:44.870
&gt;&gt; Right now I think just
for this operation it

00:19:44.871 --> 00:19:48.580
is just aggregated and
everything is stored encrypted.

00:19:48.581 --> 00:19:51.665
&gt;&gt; Okay. So if I want
to access the output I

00:19:51.666 --> 00:19:55.395
should know the position of my.

00:19:55.396 --> 00:19:57.975
&gt;&gt; So, once you're
done with performing,

00:19:57.976 --> 00:19:59.805
so here is what the program
is going to look like.

00:19:59.806 --> 00:20:01.190
It would be a sequence
of scatter, gather,

00:20:01.191 --> 00:20:03.300
and apply operations in
some particular order,

00:20:03.301 --> 00:20:05.230
and in the end if you want for

00:20:05.231 --> 00:20:07.445
example say data
corresponding to all vertices,

00:20:07.446 --> 00:20:09.320
I just need to perform
an Oblivious sort

00:20:09.321 --> 00:20:10.575
again in such a way that

00:20:10.576 --> 00:20:12.435
the vertices are at one end

00:20:12.436 --> 00:20:14.865
and then you can
reveal just that data.

00:20:14.866 --> 00:20:19.650
&gt;&gt; Then it's okay. Fine. Thanks.

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:22.830
&gt;&gt; Okay. So in general,

00:20:22.831 --> 00:20:24.220
your problem is going to
look like the following,

00:20:24.221 --> 00:20:25.390
where you have an area of

00:20:25.391 --> 00:20:27.075
indisposed white
and yellow cells.

00:20:27.076 --> 00:20:28.585
And for every yellow cell,

00:20:28.586 --> 00:20:29.970
you are going to
aggregate the data

00:20:29.971 --> 00:20:32.010
in the preceding white cells.

00:20:32.011 --> 00:20:35.010
And the goal over here is
to paralyse computation,

00:20:35.011 --> 00:20:36.510
in such a way that each of

00:20:36.511 --> 00:20:38.975
the processors performs
an equal amount of work.

00:20:38.976 --> 00:20:41.090
That is, you want to
distribute the graph evenly,

00:20:41.091 --> 00:20:43.620
so that the total amount
of work is not too high.

00:20:43.621 --> 00:20:47.110
And in addition, you'd want
to achieve Obliviousness,

00:20:47.111 --> 00:20:48.660
that is the
communication in between

00:20:48.661 --> 00:20:51.485
these processors does not
reveal any information.

00:20:51.486 --> 00:20:55.020
And we do this by dividing

00:20:55.021 --> 00:20:58.695
the array equally in between
different processors,

00:20:58.696 --> 00:21:00.940
so that the number
of vertices that

00:21:00.941 --> 00:21:03.720
one processor is
responsible does not matter.

00:21:03.721 --> 00:21:05.390
It is a submission of

00:21:05.391 --> 00:21:08.285
vertices and edges that
a processor is responsible for.

00:21:08.286 --> 00:21:11.365
And this is done in
such a way that a processor

00:21:11.366 --> 00:21:14.550
is connected to logarithmically
many other processors,

00:21:14.551 --> 00:21:16.000
and the amount of communication

00:21:16.001 --> 00:21:18.280
in between them is logarithmic.

00:21:18.281 --> 00:21:20.310
I'm not going to
go into the details

00:21:20.311 --> 00:21:21.940
of exactly how this is achieved,

00:21:21.941 --> 00:21:23.900
but this is actually

00:21:23.901 --> 00:21:26.595
a generalization of
the more well known part, sorry.

00:21:26.596 --> 00:21:29.920
&gt;&gt; Can I have a question
[inaudible] So do you do the

00:21:29.921 --> 00:21:33.710
oblivious sort on
multiple processor?

00:21:33.711 --> 00:21:34.850
You go for first,

00:21:34.851 --> 00:21:36.400
or I'll just be then?

00:21:36.401 --> 00:21:38.270
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes. We do it
on multiple processor.

00:21:38.271 --> 00:21:40.565
We implement a Bitonic sort.

00:21:40.566 --> 00:21:43.225
And because it is

00:21:43.226 --> 00:21:47.540
a network sorting algorithm,
it's easy to do that.

00:21:47.541 --> 00:21:51.370
So it turns out that problem
is actually a generalization

00:21:51.371 --> 00:21:53.745
of the more well known
parallel prefix sum problem.

00:21:53.746 --> 00:21:55.685
Then let's say you
have an input array,

00:21:55.686 --> 00:21:58.685
and the output for
the eight element,

00:21:58.686 --> 00:22:01.430
you would want the summation
of the first elements.

00:22:01.431 --> 00:22:03.195
And let us say, we have

00:22:03.196 --> 00:22:06.705
one processor
working on each set.

00:22:06.706 --> 00:22:08.710
And this can be done easily

00:22:08.711 --> 00:22:10.590
in logarithmic number of steps,

00:22:10.591 --> 00:22:11.995
where in each step,

00:22:11.996 --> 00:22:13.250
each processor is performing

00:22:13.251 --> 00:22:15.390
some sort of divide
and conquer algorithm.

00:22:15.391 --> 00:22:16.870
And this is being done in

00:22:16.871 --> 00:22:20.260
sync with all of the processors.

00:22:20.261 --> 00:22:23.390
With that, you had as well

00:22:23.391 --> 00:22:25.235
the complexity of
these operations look like.

00:22:25.236 --> 00:22:27.275
If you would have
used an ORAM baseline,

00:22:27.276 --> 00:22:31.295
it would have required
E polylog V time.

00:22:31.296 --> 00:22:33.290
Our solution for
scatter and gather

00:22:33.291 --> 00:22:35.490
can perform it in O
over E log V time.

00:22:35.491 --> 00:22:38.530
And for apply, it can
do it in O E V time.

00:22:38.531 --> 00:22:40.645
And in parallel, as
I just mentioned,

00:22:40.646 --> 00:22:42.340
the depth of
our computation is O log

00:22:42.341 --> 00:22:44.575
V. And although I did
not mention apply,

00:22:44.576 --> 00:22:47.705
the depth of that
computation is constrict.

00:22:47.706 --> 00:22:50.870
So until now, I
have assumed that

00:22:50.871 --> 00:22:53.510
we have access to a secure
processor, but turns out,

00:22:53.511 --> 00:22:55.580
we abstract the secure processor

00:22:55.581 --> 00:22:58.430
using two non-colluding
cloud service providers,

00:22:58.431 --> 00:23:01.945
and we use a secure computation
protocol in between them.

00:23:01.946 --> 00:23:03.930
So let me stick

00:23:03.931 --> 00:23:06.575
a small detail and explain
more secure computation is,

00:23:06.576 --> 00:23:09.515
and then let us see how we
can apply this in our setting.

00:23:09.516 --> 00:23:11.555
So, secure computation protocol

00:23:11.556 --> 00:23:13.780
allows two parties, or
two or more parties.

00:23:13.781 --> 00:23:15.340
In this case,
two parties. Let's say,

00:23:15.341 --> 00:23:17.570
one with input X and
the other with input Y.

00:23:17.571 --> 00:23:20.050
To compute some
function f of x_y,

00:23:20.051 --> 00:23:21.960
in such a way that one party

00:23:21.961 --> 00:23:24.345
does not reveal its input
to the other party.

00:23:24.346 --> 00:23:27.110
And if I have access
to such a protocol,

00:23:27.111 --> 00:23:31.520
let us see how we can
use this in our setting.

00:23:31.521 --> 00:23:33.555
First, for my dataset,

00:23:33.556 --> 00:23:36.560
I can encrypt this by
just secret sharing

00:23:36.561 --> 00:23:37.820
this in between two

00:23:37.821 --> 00:23:39.470
non-colluding cloud
service providers.

00:23:39.471 --> 00:23:42.370
Lets say, Netflix and Amazon.

00:23:42.371 --> 00:23:46.480
And secret sharing ensures
that each shared by

00:23:46.481 --> 00:23:48.520
itself does not
reveal any information

00:23:48.521 --> 00:23:50.185
to either Netflix or Amazon,

00:23:50.186 --> 00:23:51.770
but together, they have

00:23:51.771 --> 00:23:52.690
all the information that is

00:23:52.691 --> 00:23:55.265
necessary to perform
the computation.

00:23:55.266 --> 00:23:57.540
And now, they can just run

00:23:57.541 --> 00:23:59.600
a secure computation
protocol with

00:23:59.601 --> 00:24:02.860
the algorithm that I
just described earlier.

00:24:02.861 --> 00:24:05.190
And this ensures
that Netflix never

00:24:05.191 --> 00:24:08.360
learns Amazon share,
and vice versa.

00:24:08.361 --> 00:24:10.245
And that means, they can
learn the output without

00:24:10.246 --> 00:24:13.155
knowing the input ahead of time.

00:24:13.156 --> 00:24:15.535
And this ensures that

00:24:15.536 --> 00:24:17.650
your data on vertices
and edges is hidden,

00:24:17.651 --> 00:24:19.920
and the graph structure
results are hidden.

00:24:19.921 --> 00:24:22.825
So for a specific
implementation,

00:24:22.826 --> 00:24:26.170
we used Yao's garbled circuits.

00:24:26.171 --> 00:24:28.670
And we build this
on top of FlexSC,

00:24:28.671 --> 00:24:32.780
which is ObliVM's backend.

00:24:32.781 --> 00:24:34.070
And ObliVM is a secure

00:24:34.071 --> 00:24:36.300
computation framework we
were working on at that time.

00:24:36.301 --> 00:24:38.100
In case you haven't
heard of Yao's

00:24:38.101 --> 00:24:39.750
garbled circuit, that is fine.

00:24:39.751 --> 00:24:42.740
It is just a secure computation
protocol that we used.

00:24:42.741 --> 00:24:50.035
So given parallelism was
one of our key improvements,

00:24:50.036 --> 00:24:52.050
the first thing we wanted to do

00:24:52.051 --> 00:24:55.775
was try to run this on as
bigger computation as we can.

00:24:55.776 --> 00:24:57.230
And we did this for the task

00:24:57.231 --> 00:24:58.790
of computing user
recommendations.

00:24:58.791 --> 00:25:00.470
And the algorithm
that we implemented

00:25:00.471 --> 00:25:03.680
was sparse matrix factorization
using gradient design.

00:25:03.681 --> 00:25:05.280
And we could do this

00:25:05.281 --> 00:25:09.025
on a MovieLens dataset
of size one million,

00:25:09.026 --> 00:25:10.750
where when I say one million,

00:25:10.751 --> 00:25:12.315
I mean the number of
vertices and number of

00:25:12.316 --> 00:25:14.995
edges in this graph
together is one million.

00:25:14.996 --> 00:25:16.970
And the size of the computation

00:25:16.971 --> 00:25:18.955
was about a trillion
garbled gates.

00:25:18.956 --> 00:25:20.560
And at the time,

00:25:20.561 --> 00:25:21.870
this was the largest
ever computation

00:25:21.871 --> 00:25:23.960
that was performed using
secure computation.

00:25:23.961 --> 00:25:27.855
And this would then
using 128 CPU cores,

00:25:27.856 --> 00:25:31.625
which is what we had access
to in our lab at that time.

00:25:31.626 --> 00:25:36.045
Comparing this to prior work,

00:25:36.046 --> 00:25:38.470
they could do
this computation only

00:25:38.471 --> 00:25:41.125
on size 16,000 ratings.

00:25:41.126 --> 00:25:44.360
And for the same size
dataset using 32 CPU cores,

00:25:44.361 --> 00:25:46.020
what took them one point for us

00:25:46.021 --> 00:25:47.985
took us less than three
and a half minutes.

00:25:47.986 --> 00:25:50.290
And this 20x improvement is

00:25:50.291 --> 00:25:53.365
primarily because of the VVA
efficiently parallelism.

00:25:53.366 --> 00:25:55.275
But in addition to parallelism,

00:25:55.276 --> 00:25:58.090
we do this generically for
any program that can be

00:25:58.091 --> 00:26:02.440
expressed in the
graph parallel state.

00:26:02.441 --> 00:26:04.440
And for them, they
just handcrafted

00:26:04.441 --> 00:26:08.215
this computation for
this specific example.

00:26:08.216 --> 00:26:11.340
&gt;&gt; Can I as a question?

00:26:11.341 --> 00:26:11.551
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:26:11.551 --> 00:26:16.780
&gt;&gt; If the number from
one million become 10 million,

00:26:16.781 --> 00:26:21.370
how do the computation in
terms of CPU, memory usage?

00:26:21.371 --> 00:26:24.210
&gt;&gt; So, in terms of time.

00:26:24.211 --> 00:26:25.780
&gt;&gt; Time, yeah.

00:26:25.781 --> 00:26:27.300
&gt;&gt; In terms of
time, it would be,

00:26:27.301 --> 00:26:31.995
so it behaves as n locks
squared m. So it would be

00:26:31.996 --> 00:26:34.510
almost linear but that
there would be a lock

00:26:34.511 --> 00:26:37.630
squared component that would
also come into picture.

00:26:37.631 --> 00:26:40.985
&gt;&gt; What about CPU usage
and the memory?

00:26:40.986 --> 00:26:43.220
&gt;&gt; So when we implemented this,

00:26:43.221 --> 00:26:44.805
this was CPU bound.

00:26:44.806 --> 00:26:46.410
Right now it is communication

00:26:46.411 --> 00:26:50.355
bound because with
newer algorithms,

00:26:50.356 --> 00:26:53.680
newer processors have access to

00:26:53.681 --> 00:26:56.990
ASNI because of these
computations are now faster,

00:26:56.991 --> 00:26:58.680
and now it is
communication bound.

00:26:58.681 --> 00:27:02.525
So in terms of
computational resources,

00:27:02.526 --> 00:27:04.900
I'm saying it would take
that much amount of time

00:27:04.901 --> 00:27:06.250
using the same amount
of computational

00:27:06.251 --> 00:27:08.065
resources that we're
using over here.

00:27:08.066 --> 00:27:10.800
If we can have higher amount
of computation of resources,

00:27:10.801 --> 00:27:12.990
which I'm going to get to next,

00:27:12.991 --> 00:27:15.440
then you can still have
possibly the same amount

00:27:15.441 --> 00:27:16.730
of time depending on

00:27:16.731 --> 00:27:20.335
how many resources you
use. So, let us see how.

00:27:20.336 --> 00:27:23.470
&gt;&gt; Sorry, I didn't follow
the switch from like talking

00:27:23.471 --> 00:27:24.790
about ORAM and

00:27:24.791 --> 00:27:27.535
parallel computation
to secure computation.

00:27:27.536 --> 00:27:28.090
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:27:28.091 --> 00:27:30.450
&gt;&gt; So, why was that necessary?

00:27:30.451 --> 00:27:31.860
This might be
a dumb question. So, I

00:27:31.861 --> 00:27:33.325
mean I just didn't
follow that switch.

00:27:33.326 --> 00:27:35.270
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, the same

00:27:35.271 --> 00:27:37.535
result we have apply
in both these settings.

00:27:37.536 --> 00:27:39.695
One, where you have

00:27:39.696 --> 00:27:41.590
a secure processor
and you're trying

00:27:41.591 --> 00:27:43.090
to hide memory access patterns.

00:27:43.091 --> 00:27:45.535
In terms of in
secure computation,

00:27:45.536 --> 00:27:47.070
the problem is
slightly different

00:27:47.071 --> 00:27:49.745
where you have a ramp program

00:27:49.746 --> 00:27:51.500
and eventually
you want a socket.

00:27:51.501 --> 00:27:53.560
And an important
intermediary over

00:27:53.561 --> 00:27:55.790
here is the notion of
an oblivious program.

00:27:55.791 --> 00:27:58.645
So, the place where this ORAM or

00:27:58.646 --> 00:28:01.080
these oblivious algorithms
come into picture is

00:28:01.081 --> 00:28:02.340
their design of these oblivious

00:28:02.341 --> 00:28:03.795
algorithms which
is the hard part.

00:28:03.796 --> 00:28:05.965
And once you have
an oblivious algorithm,

00:28:05.966 --> 00:28:08.035
converting it into circuits is

00:28:08.036 --> 00:28:15.945
an easy translation.
Well, I'm not sure if I.

00:28:15.946 --> 00:28:21.810
&gt;&gt; Im just going to ask a sort
of related question but.

00:28:21.811 --> 00:28:22.546
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; Again.

00:28:22.546 --> 00:28:24.110
So, when you're talking
about the oblivious sort,

00:28:24.111 --> 00:28:27.190
for example, do you still
need ORAM or not anymore?

00:28:27.191 --> 00:28:28.800
&gt;&gt; No.
&gt;&gt; Or you'd like custom,

00:28:28.801 --> 00:28:31.285
or basically looking
for a good sort?

00:28:31.286 --> 00:28:33.735
&gt;&gt; We are just trying
to get rid of an ORAM.

00:28:33.736 --> 00:28:36.875
We are not using an ORAM
altogether, yeah.

00:28:36.876 --> 00:28:38.500
&gt;&gt; You don't want ORAM?

00:28:38.501 --> 00:28:40.175
&gt;&gt; Yeah. We are just trying
to make it oblivious,

00:28:40.176 --> 00:28:41.860
and Obliviousness and, achieving

00:28:41.861 --> 00:28:44.920
Obliviousness and writing
a circuit, they're synonymous.

00:28:44.921 --> 00:28:46.960
So, once you have
the Obliviousness,

00:28:46.961 --> 00:28:54.850
converting it into a circuit
is a one-to-one translation.

00:28:54.851 --> 00:28:56.710
&gt;&gt; I will look into it later.

00:28:56.711 --> 00:29:05.335
&gt;&gt; Sure. Okay. So, in
terms of parallelism,

00:29:05.336 --> 00:29:07.960
so let us say, this is
the high level result.

00:29:07.961 --> 00:29:10.050
On the Y axis you'll
see the execution time,

00:29:10.051 --> 00:29:12.620
and on the X axis you'll see
the number of processors.

00:29:12.621 --> 00:29:15.060
And both of these access
are log scaled.

00:29:15.061 --> 00:29:16.910
And the different lines over

00:29:16.911 --> 00:29:19.470
here correspond to
different graph sizes.

00:29:19.471 --> 00:29:21.300
And as you can see, as

00:29:21.301 --> 00:29:22.830
we increase the number
of processors,

00:29:22.831 --> 00:29:24.445
the computation time reduces.

00:29:24.446 --> 00:29:25.860
And although it is not clear

00:29:25.861 --> 00:29:27.585
because this is a log log scale,

00:29:27.586 --> 00:29:29.210
it is actually true that as you

00:29:29.211 --> 00:29:30.510
increase the number
of processors,

00:29:30.511 --> 00:29:33.890
their decrease in competition
time is almost linear.

00:29:33.891 --> 00:29:36.845
And finally, just because
we had a framework,

00:29:36.846 --> 00:29:38.340
we did this for
a bunch of algorithms

00:29:38.341 --> 00:29:40.195
and not just for
one specific example.

00:29:40.196 --> 00:29:44.040
And the same result holds
for all of these examples.

00:29:44.041 --> 00:29:47.870
So with that, I discuss GraphSC,

00:29:47.871 --> 00:29:49.590
where you can take
programs written

00:29:49.591 --> 00:29:51.720
in these obstructions of
scatter, gather, and apply.

00:29:51.721 --> 00:29:55.590
And you can convert them
efficiently into- and

00:29:55.591 --> 00:29:57.340
GraphSC can work
them efficiently into

00:29:57.341 --> 00:30:00.560
a parallel execution
using secure computation.

00:30:00.561 --> 00:30:03.640
With that, let me move
on to the second part

00:30:03.641 --> 00:30:07.065
of my talk on
obfuscating programs.

00:30:07.066 --> 00:30:10.545
Don't solve the problem
of achieving

00:30:10.546 --> 00:30:13.400
obfuscation is relatively old

00:30:13.401 --> 00:30:15.680
and there has been a lot
of approaches to do this.

00:30:15.681 --> 00:30:18.490
There have been heuristic
approaches to obfuscation.

00:30:18.491 --> 00:30:21.285
For example, this
is one example.

00:30:21.286 --> 00:30:24.460
And I have a question for you.
What does this program do?

00:30:24.461 --> 00:30:28.870
Or for that matter, what
does this program look like?

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:36.890
Any guesses? I think if
you squint your eyes,

00:30:36.891 --> 00:30:39.160
or if you go further
behind, perhaps,

00:30:39.161 --> 00:30:44.380
it would be easier. Sorry?

00:30:44.381 --> 00:30:45.425
&gt;&gt; It's flappy bird.

00:30:45.426 --> 00:30:50.410
&gt;&gt; Yes. So this
is a flappy bird.

00:30:50.411 --> 00:30:52.060
So a game that was very famous

00:30:52.061 --> 00:30:53.540
I think a couple of years ago.

00:30:53.541 --> 00:30:55.680
In terms of, if you
execute this program,

00:30:55.681 --> 00:30:58.615
you can play flappy bird
on your dominant.

00:30:58.616 --> 00:31:02.420
So, such approaches to

00:31:02.421 --> 00:31:04.480
obfuscation tend to
be efficient but they

00:31:04.481 --> 00:31:09.360
actually give you no guarantees
on what it obfuscates.

00:31:09.361 --> 00:31:12.410
And at the other end it is
known that it is impossible to

00:31:12.411 --> 00:31:14.270
achieve software only program

00:31:14.271 --> 00:31:16.095
obfuscation in a strong sense.

00:31:16.096 --> 00:31:20.690
So given this, we are trying
to come up with a scheme

00:31:20.691 --> 00:31:25.755
for obfuscation by using
some other assumptions.

00:31:25.756 --> 00:31:27.750
There have been approaches in

00:31:27.751 --> 00:31:28.955
cryptography to achieve this.

00:31:28.956 --> 00:31:30.260
For example, they
have introduced

00:31:30.261 --> 00:31:32.935
this notion of
indistinguishably obfuscation,

00:31:32.936 --> 00:31:34.645
which is at this point

00:31:34.646 --> 00:31:37.340
inefficient and not
strong enough in practice.

00:31:37.341 --> 00:31:39.220
They have introduced this notion

00:31:39.221 --> 00:31:40.940
of using secure hardware or

00:31:40.941 --> 00:31:44.010
trusted hardware tokens,
but turns out,

00:31:44.011 --> 00:31:45.570
most of these schemes use

00:31:45.571 --> 00:31:46.880
strong cryptography
primitives such

00:31:46.881 --> 00:31:48.340
as fully homomorphic encryption,

00:31:48.341 --> 00:31:50.370
or non interactive
zero knowledge proofs,

00:31:50.371 --> 00:31:52.395
or they just try to obfuscate

00:31:52.396 --> 00:31:56.710
boolean programs
instead of LAN programs.

00:31:56.711 --> 00:31:58.700
There have been approaches in

00:31:58.701 --> 00:32:00.320
the architecture community but

00:32:00.321 --> 00:32:03.005
most of these approaches
are used to hide inputs

00:32:03.006 --> 00:32:05.600
and assume public
programs and many of

00:32:05.601 --> 00:32:07.800
these Architectural solutions

00:32:07.801 --> 00:32:10.275
actually reveal access buttons.

00:32:10.276 --> 00:32:14.205
Given that, our goal was to,

00:32:14.206 --> 00:32:16.410
our goal in HOP,
which stands for

00:32:16.411 --> 00:32:17.925
Hardware makes
Obfuscation Practical,

00:32:17.926 --> 00:32:21.090
was to achieve an efficient
obfuscation scheme using

00:32:21.091 --> 00:32:22.960
a trusted processor and

00:32:22.961 --> 00:32:26.395
the prototype hardware system
to do the same.

00:32:26.396 --> 00:32:28.240
In this process, we introduce

00:32:28.241 --> 00:32:30.730
some optimizations which
allowed us to perform up

00:32:30.731 --> 00:32:33.130
to 240X better than
a baseline scheme while

00:32:33.131 --> 00:32:36.485
still being up to 76X lower
than an in secure system.

00:32:36.486 --> 00:32:40.535
Before I explain what
our obfuscation scheme is,

00:32:40.536 --> 00:32:42.010
let me quickly define exactly

00:32:42.011 --> 00:32:44.500
what security properties
we are looking for.

00:32:44.501 --> 00:32:46.935
And let us say,

00:32:46.936 --> 00:32:48.940
in this ideal world where we

00:32:48.941 --> 00:32:50.750
have access to
a trusted third party,

00:32:50.751 --> 00:32:52.280
we have Widong who has

00:32:52.281 --> 00:32:54.985
a program he would
like to obfuscate.

00:32:54.986 --> 00:32:56.315
He wants everyone to use it but

00:32:56.316 --> 00:32:58.425
not learn what
this program is doing.

00:32:58.426 --> 00:33:01.420
And let us say,

00:33:01.421 --> 00:33:02.630
Jonathan is trying to

00:33:02.631 --> 00:33:05.155
execute this program
on his own inputs.

00:33:05.156 --> 00:33:07.120
So all Jonathan will do,

00:33:07.121 --> 00:33:08.460
so all Widong will do is

00:33:08.461 --> 00:33:10.455
send this program to
the trusted third party,

00:33:10.456 --> 00:33:12.910
and Jonathan will
just send his input to

00:33:12.911 --> 00:33:14.320
the trusted third party
which will execute

00:33:14.321 --> 00:33:16.535
this and give a result back.

00:33:16.536 --> 00:33:18.510
And let's say, in
addition to the output,

00:33:18.511 --> 00:33:20.820
it also reveals size
of the program and

00:33:20.821 --> 00:33:22.680
some execution time which is

00:33:22.681 --> 00:33:25.510
fixed for all inputs
of the same size.

00:33:25.511 --> 00:33:27.890
The reason why we're
thinking of Jonathan

00:33:27.891 --> 00:33:29.110
as being the
adversary over here,

00:33:29.111 --> 00:33:30.200
is because he's the one who's

00:33:30.201 --> 00:33:31.535
trying to learn the program.

00:33:31.536 --> 00:33:33.100
And in the ideal world,

00:33:33.101 --> 00:33:35.670
he never has access to
the program because it

00:33:35.671 --> 00:33:38.590
is a trusted third party who
has access to the program.

00:33:38.591 --> 00:33:41.955
Jonathan only has an oracle
access to the program.

00:33:41.956 --> 00:33:45.080
In the real world, sorry.

00:33:45.081 --> 00:33:46.940
And we want this,

00:33:46.941 --> 00:33:48.485
he want to ensure this for

00:33:48.486 --> 00:33:51.060
all inputs that
Jonathan may have.

00:33:51.061 --> 00:33:53.735
In the real world instead,

00:33:53.736 --> 00:33:56.510
you have this scenario
where you have

00:33:56.511 --> 00:33:57.910
a secure processor
which may have

00:33:57.911 --> 00:33:59.640
a small amount of
process storage,

00:33:59.641 --> 00:34:03.550
and it stores everything
outside in an encrypted form.

00:34:03.551 --> 00:34:05.690
And in this new world,

00:34:05.691 --> 00:34:07.320
what you can do is,

00:34:07.321 --> 00:34:09.875
Widong can send his program

00:34:09.876 --> 00:34:11.580
in an encrypted form
to Jonathan,

00:34:11.581 --> 00:34:13.810
and Jonathan can just send

00:34:13.811 --> 00:34:16.465
the program and just input
the trusted processor,

00:34:16.466 --> 00:34:21.215
and which will perform an
execution with the main memory,

00:34:21.216 --> 00:34:23.500
and finally return out.

00:34:23.501 --> 00:34:26.165
We're going to assume
that the adversary has

00:34:26.166 --> 00:34:28.885
access to this entire memory
all throughout,

00:34:28.886 --> 00:34:30.940
and in such a way that he

00:34:30.941 --> 00:34:32.480
can modify parts of memory if he

00:34:32.481 --> 00:34:34.480
likes because we're envisioning

00:34:34.481 --> 00:34:36.795
this competition to be local.

00:34:36.796 --> 00:34:40.170
And what you want is the kind of

00:34:40.171 --> 00:34:41.380
obfuscation guarantee
that you get to

00:34:41.381 --> 00:34:43.610
the left to be the same
as the one on the right.

00:34:43.611 --> 00:34:46.780
&gt;&gt; Now who encrypts the input?

00:34:46.781 --> 00:34:47.730
&gt;&gt; No one encrypts the input

00:34:47.731 --> 00:34:49.380
because it is
the adversary who's

00:34:49.381 --> 00:34:52.440
trying to run
this program on his input.

00:34:52.441 --> 00:34:55.375
&gt;&gt; I was confused by
the lock on the inputs.

00:34:55.376 --> 00:34:57.295
So you're saying, oh,
it's probably that.

00:34:57.296 --> 00:35:03.785
&gt;&gt; All right. And also,

00:35:03.786 --> 00:35:05.370
assumptions are the form where

00:35:05.371 --> 00:35:07.050
such a trusted
processor can store

00:35:07.051 --> 00:35:08.730
state in between invocations

00:35:08.731 --> 00:35:10.540
actually matters for security.

00:35:10.541 --> 00:35:12.490
So first we're
going to assume that

00:35:12.491 --> 00:35:14.200
such a trusted processor can

00:35:14.201 --> 00:35:15.870
store state in between
invocations that

00:35:15.871 --> 00:35:17.865
as we have a stateful processor.

00:35:17.866 --> 00:35:19.570
And let us try to construct

00:35:19.571 --> 00:35:22.170
an obfuscation scheme
in this scenario.

00:35:22.171 --> 00:35:24.750
So whenever some execution is

00:35:24.751 --> 00:35:26.360
happening at
every point in time,

00:35:26.361 --> 00:35:28.320
the processor is going
to read an instruction.

00:35:28.321 --> 00:35:32.330
And this instruction can be
a simple move instruction,

00:35:32.331 --> 00:35:33.540
or an arithmetic instruction,

00:35:33.541 --> 00:35:35.755
or it can actually
be a memory access.

00:35:35.756 --> 00:35:37.870
And whether or not, it
does a memory access

00:35:37.871 --> 00:35:40.240
this is something that is
available to the adversary.

00:35:40.241 --> 00:35:42.470
So the first thing you would
want to do is you would

00:35:42.471 --> 00:35:44.930
want to hide whether it's
a memory access or not.

00:35:44.931 --> 00:35:47.420
And one way to do this is to

00:35:47.421 --> 00:35:50.060
ensure that for
every non-memory access,

00:35:50.061 --> 00:35:53.440
you actually perform
memory accesses with.

00:35:53.441 --> 00:35:56.690
Second, based on what
I've been saying earlier,

00:35:56.691 --> 00:35:58.840
we would want to protect
our access patterns

00:35:58.841 --> 00:36:01.060
and use an oblivious
RAM over here.

00:36:01.061 --> 00:36:03.440
Third, because the adversary

00:36:03.441 --> 00:36:05.070
has access to the entire memory,

00:36:05.071 --> 00:36:06.895
and he can modify parts of it,

00:36:06.896 --> 00:36:08.390
your trusted processor would

00:36:08.391 --> 00:36:09.450
want to authenticate memory at

00:36:09.451 --> 00:36:10.470
all points in time to

00:36:10.471 --> 00:36:12.040
ensure that the memory
is not modified.

00:36:12.041 --> 00:36:15.195
And it would keep an
authentication token within itself.

00:36:15.196 --> 00:36:17.820
And finally, you
would want to run

00:36:17.821 --> 00:36:20.450
this program for a fixed time T
for all inputs of

00:36:20.451 --> 00:36:23.115
same size just so that
the adversary does not

00:36:23.116 --> 00:36:24.650
learn how the program

00:36:24.651 --> 00:36:26.995
behaves for different inputs
of the same size.

00:36:26.996 --> 00:36:29.345
And this is what I'm
going to refer to as

00:36:29.346 --> 00:36:32.080
a baseline scheme later
on in my evaluation.

00:36:32.081 --> 00:36:35.965
And it turns out
the few do this,

00:36:35.966 --> 00:36:37.060
you will achieve some notion of

00:36:37.061 --> 00:36:40.095
obfuscation if you're
running a single application.

00:36:40.096 --> 00:36:42.330
If you're running
multiple applications,

00:36:42.331 --> 00:36:44.640
let's say, concurrently,
in that case,

00:36:44.641 --> 00:36:46.480
the state corresponding to

00:36:46.481 --> 00:36:48.740
one program needs
to move out of the

00:36:48.741 --> 00:36:50.555
token so that it
can feed it with

00:36:50.556 --> 00:36:53.485
the state corresponding
to the other programs.

00:36:53.486 --> 00:36:55.540
And such contact switching

00:36:55.541 --> 00:36:59.710
motivates the formulation
of a stateless processor.

00:36:59.711 --> 00:37:02.870
Okay. A stateless
processor is something

00:37:02.871 --> 00:37:04.850
that does not maintain
state between invocations.

00:37:04.851 --> 00:37:06.620
So at the end of the invocation,

00:37:06.621 --> 00:37:09.810
it will give out an encrypted
state that it stores,

00:37:09.811 --> 00:37:11.490
and it would expect
the adversary to send

00:37:11.491 --> 00:37:13.920
the state back at
the next invocation.

00:37:13.921 --> 00:37:16.605
And if I have such a
stateless processor,

00:37:16.606 --> 00:37:18.640
even it's something that
an adversary can do.

00:37:18.641 --> 00:37:21.345
He will execute
the first 10 instructions.

00:37:21.346 --> 00:37:23.040
And now, it is the turn
of the adversary

00:37:23.041 --> 00:37:24.930
to execute the
eleventh instructions.

00:37:24.931 --> 00:37:26.805
But the adversary will send

00:37:26.806 --> 00:37:28.905
the state corresponding to
the tenth instruction again,

00:37:28.906 --> 00:37:29.760
and make the processor

00:37:29.761 --> 00:37:31.625
execute the tenth
instruction again.

00:37:31.626 --> 00:37:34.520
And based on two executions
of the tenth instruction,

00:37:34.521 --> 00:37:36.705
it will try to learn
more information.

00:37:36.706 --> 00:37:38.560
And turns out such an attack

00:37:38.561 --> 00:37:40.445
which we call a
rewinding attack...

00:37:40.446 --> 00:37:42.490
In such an attack
in the scenario,

00:37:42.491 --> 00:37:44.495
an adversary can actually
learn information.

00:37:44.496 --> 00:37:46.480
And the problem is that,

00:37:46.481 --> 00:37:48.300
oblivious RAMs are not secured

00:37:48.301 --> 00:37:51.560
against such
rewinding adversaries.

00:37:51.561 --> 00:37:54.120
Because I have not explained

00:37:54.121 --> 00:37:56.850
an oblivious RAM scheme
as such in this talk,

00:37:56.851 --> 00:37:58.370
I will not go into the details

00:37:58.371 --> 00:38:01.425
of the exact attack
in model we're here.

00:38:01.426 --> 00:38:04.010
But what I will
still give you is in

00:38:04.011 --> 00:38:07.585
detail but in precise
explanation of why that happens.

00:38:07.586 --> 00:38:10.340
Let us say, my memory location

00:38:10.341 --> 00:38:11.870
of interest is
memory location five,

00:38:11.871 --> 00:38:15.015
and the two obfuscating accesses
are two, seven and two.

00:38:15.016 --> 00:38:18.225
If I rewind execution
and if I run it again,

00:38:18.226 --> 00:38:20.155
I still need to access

00:38:20.156 --> 00:38:21.410
memory location
five because that is

00:38:21.411 --> 00:38:22.955
needed for my [inaudible]
as guarantees,

00:38:22.956 --> 00:38:24.590
but the two obfuscating accesses

00:38:24.591 --> 00:38:26.445
maybe two to
different locations.

00:38:26.446 --> 00:38:28.700
And if I rewind, if I keep

00:38:28.701 --> 00:38:29.900
rewinding and if I just perform

00:38:29.901 --> 00:38:31.195
a certain to section operation,

00:38:31.196 --> 00:38:32.450
then I will exactly know

00:38:32.451 --> 00:38:34.995
which memory location
you're accessing.

00:38:34.996 --> 00:38:37.100
And although oblivious RAMs

00:38:37.101 --> 00:38:38.800
are used to hide
memory access patterns,

00:38:38.801 --> 00:38:41.240
we are not achieving
that at all.

00:38:41.241 --> 00:38:43.970
&gt;&gt; So remind me
again, why do you want

00:38:43.971 --> 00:38:46.430
to contact stateless process?

00:38:46.431 --> 00:38:47.980
Is it because you don't want to

00:38:47.981 --> 00:38:49.990
assume that
the entire computation

00:38:49.991 --> 00:38:51.710
is only one goal the adversary

00:38:51.711 --> 00:38:53.900
can put the machine in between?

00:38:53.901 --> 00:38:54.980
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:38:54.981 --> 00:38:56.730
&gt;&gt; Why is that
a practical concept?

00:38:56.731 --> 00:38:58.370
Like if the adversary

00:38:58.371 --> 00:39:00.055
repushed just start
from the beginning and,

00:39:00.056 --> 00:39:01.670
like what kind of
programs are you thinking?

00:39:01.671 --> 00:39:04.585
Like are you just
allowed any programs?

00:39:04.586 --> 00:39:08.100
&gt;&gt; Any program that may
be long running because

00:39:08.101 --> 00:39:09.740
such computations
tend to be much

00:39:09.741 --> 00:39:11.535
slower than
existing computations.

00:39:11.536 --> 00:39:13.355
So if you want to
context switch,

00:39:13.356 --> 00:39:15.975
one way to do this is to
ensure that your processor is

00:39:15.976 --> 00:39:19.145
stateful and it remembers
context of what's outside.

00:39:19.146 --> 00:39:22.500
&gt;&gt; All I need to remember
is who contact, right?

00:39:22.501 --> 00:39:23.780
So in case I need to write

00:39:23.781 --> 00:39:25.630
my state as [inaudible]
keep track of progress.

00:39:25.631 --> 00:39:28.000
&gt;&gt; Yes. Precisely. So if you can

00:39:28.001 --> 00:39:30.985
maintain that and if you
have a stateful processor,

00:39:30.986 --> 00:39:32.830
well and good, you can do that.

00:39:32.831 --> 00:39:34.025
But what we show is that,

00:39:34.026 --> 00:39:35.750
even if you do
not remember or if

00:39:35.751 --> 00:39:37.600
your processor does not
remember this information,

00:39:37.601 --> 00:39:42.505
you can still achieve
the same guarantees.

00:39:42.506 --> 00:39:45.380
&gt;&gt; In this specific algorithm,

00:39:45.381 --> 00:39:48.290
can you fix it just
by using PRG cable?

00:39:48.291 --> 00:39:51.365
&gt;&gt; That is perfect.

00:39:51.366 --> 00:39:53.985
&gt;&gt; Are there other attacks

00:39:53.986 --> 00:39:56.170
that this doesn't fix? It
doesn't have [inaudible]

00:39:56.171 --> 00:39:59.495
&gt;&gt; This fixes it, yeah.

00:39:59.496 --> 00:40:02.800
So, specifically
over here we make

00:40:02.801 --> 00:40:04.610
sure that adversity commits

00:40:04.611 --> 00:40:06.225
to his program and
input ahead of time.

00:40:06.226 --> 00:40:08.660
And then, you use
your randomness based on

00:40:08.661 --> 00:40:10.020
a pseudo-random function
that depends

00:40:10.021 --> 00:40:12.435
on the program and the input.

00:40:12.436 --> 00:40:14.880
And in this case,
even if you rewind,

00:40:14.881 --> 00:40:16.250
you will still to make sure

00:40:16.251 --> 00:40:19.380
that you use the same
obfuscating accesses.

00:40:19.381 --> 00:40:21.530
So, with that, I've
explained some of

00:40:21.531 --> 00:40:23.635
the key elements involved
in this construction.

00:40:23.636 --> 00:40:25.600
Let me move on to
quickly describing

00:40:25.601 --> 00:40:27.130
the optimizations and quickly

00:40:27.131 --> 00:40:29.330
describing the architecture
of the system.

00:40:29.331 --> 00:40:32.290
So, we have two
optimizations over here.

00:40:32.291 --> 00:40:34.330
The first optimization
tries to reduce

00:40:34.331 --> 00:40:36.725
the number of memory locations
that you're accessing.

00:40:36.726 --> 00:40:40.280
And this we need to do because

00:40:40.281 --> 00:40:41.905
ORAM accesses tend to be

00:40:41.906 --> 00:40:44.720
expensive compared to
a arithmetic instructions.

00:40:44.721 --> 00:40:46.980
At a high level over here,

00:40:46.981 --> 00:40:48.480
we're just trying to interleave

00:40:48.481 --> 00:40:50.685
the number of arithmetic
and memory accesses.

00:40:50.686 --> 00:40:53.460
And the second optimization
over here tries to

00:40:53.461 --> 00:40:54.590
use a small amount of

00:40:54.591 --> 00:40:57.435
trusted storage within
the secured processor.

00:40:57.436 --> 00:41:00.585
And if you have programs
that exhibit locality,

00:41:00.586 --> 00:41:02.790
you would encode a scratchpad

00:41:02.791 --> 00:41:06.465
access instead of
a main memory access.

00:41:06.466 --> 00:41:08.970
So, here is what

00:41:08.971 --> 00:41:10.450
the architecture of
the system looks like.

00:41:10.451 --> 00:41:12.170
We have a secure co-processor,

00:41:12.171 --> 00:41:15.020
which works in conjunction
with the host processor,

00:41:15.021 --> 00:41:18.275
which is the adversity, and

00:41:18.276 --> 00:41:21.030
your data is stored
in an ORAM memory,

00:41:21.031 --> 00:41:22.940
which is encrypted.

00:41:22.941 --> 00:41:28.030
We started off with

00:41:28.031 --> 00:41:30.150
the very local corresponding
to a RISC-V processor,

00:41:30.151 --> 00:41:31.610
and we modified this to

00:41:31.611 --> 00:41:33.915
include a scratchpad
load instruction,

00:41:33.916 --> 00:41:35.195
which was used to fill

00:41:35.196 --> 00:41:37.165
the data and
instruction scratchpad.

00:41:37.166 --> 00:41:38.890
For an Oblivious RAM,

00:41:38.891 --> 00:41:42.300
we used a variant of path
ORAM called Freecursive ORAM,

00:41:42.301 --> 00:41:45.190
and this is something
that was built previously.

00:41:45.191 --> 00:41:47.880
And finally, we had encryption
and decryption units to

00:41:47.881 --> 00:41:51.280
ensure that all the data
outside is stored encrypted.

00:41:51.281 --> 00:41:55.005
And here's what the higher
level result looks like.

00:41:55.006 --> 00:41:58.680
On the y-axis, you have
Slowdown to Insecure Execution.

00:41:58.681 --> 00:42:00.770
And on the x-axis, you
have different programs

00:42:00.771 --> 00:42:03.140
that we have implemented.

00:42:03.141 --> 00:42:05.900
And the two bars referred to

00:42:05.901 --> 00:42:09.260
the baseline scheme and
to our construction.

00:42:09.261 --> 00:42:10.500
So, baseline scheme
is something that I

00:42:10.501 --> 00:42:12.060
described much earlier and,

00:42:12.061 --> 00:42:14.625
HOP is baseline with
the two optimizations.

00:42:14.626 --> 00:42:16.410
And the high level result is

00:42:16.411 --> 00:42:18.495
that correspondent
to baseline scheme,

00:42:18.496 --> 00:42:20.890
we perform up to 240 expected.

00:42:20.891 --> 00:42:23.380
And just so that we
can see the y-axis and

00:42:23.381 --> 00:42:25.580
Slowdown to Insecure Execution
of HOP clearly,

00:42:25.581 --> 00:42:28.020
let me move this to
a logarithmic scale.

00:42:28.021 --> 00:42:30.605
So, the y-axis is
now logarithmic.

00:42:30.606 --> 00:42:32.160
And the high level
result over here is

00:42:32.161 --> 00:42:34.080
that compared to
insecure execution,

00:42:34.081 --> 00:42:37.860
we perform in the range
of 8x - 76x HOPs.

00:42:37.861 --> 00:42:40.680
So, programs such
as Binary Search,

00:42:40.681 --> 00:42:42.610
which exhibit
low degree of locality,

00:42:42.611 --> 00:42:45.345
they perform upto 76x HOPs.

00:42:45.346 --> 00:42:47.490
But for programs such as
[inaudible] transform,

00:42:47.491 --> 00:42:49.320
which have a high degree
of locality,

00:42:49.321 --> 00:42:52.170
they perform to 8x HOPs.

00:42:52.171 --> 00:42:56.260
With that, I've discussed
a secure obfuscation scheme,

00:42:56.261 --> 00:42:57.610
which has been designed

00:42:57.611 --> 00:42:59.985
with architectural
constraints in mind.

00:42:59.986 --> 00:43:04.510
One key addition that is
necessary in this space is

00:43:04.511 --> 00:43:07.040
some programming language
support so that the developer

00:43:07.041 --> 00:43:09.805
can easily specify how
to use a scratchpad,

00:43:09.806 --> 00:43:11.890
or hopefully, it's
not going to be

00:43:11.891 --> 00:43:16.135
the developer's concern on
how the scratchpad is used.

00:43:16.136 --> 00:43:19.850
So, with that, I have spoken

00:43:19.851 --> 00:43:21.370
about my work on computing on

00:43:21.371 --> 00:43:23.460
private data and
obfuscation programs.

00:43:23.461 --> 00:43:25.540
Let me try to speak

00:43:25.541 --> 00:43:28.105
about my research a
little more in general.

00:43:28.106 --> 00:43:30.835
My research has focused
broadly in two areas.

00:43:30.836 --> 00:43:32.675
One is on privacy-preserving
computation

00:43:32.676 --> 00:43:35.150
and second has been
towards Blockchains.

00:43:35.151 --> 00:43:37.030
In privacy-preserving
computation,

00:43:37.031 --> 00:43:38.480
I spoke about vandalism in

00:43:38.481 --> 00:43:42.220
Oblivious execution and
obfuscated program execution.

00:43:42.221 --> 00:43:45.085
In the first section,
I have worked towards

00:43:45.086 --> 00:43:46.910
a bunch of papers

00:43:46.911 --> 00:43:49.475
on Oblivious RAMs and
Oblivious programs.

00:43:49.476 --> 00:43:50.860
For example, I
have worked towards

00:43:50.861 --> 00:43:52.365
designing an
Oblivious RAM scheme,

00:43:52.366 --> 00:43:54.530
which actually achieves
the Goldriech-Ostrovsky lower

00:43:54.531 --> 00:43:56.680
bound although we actually

00:43:56.681 --> 00:43:57.900
changed one of the assumptions,

00:43:57.901 --> 00:43:59.125
that we assume

00:43:59.126 --> 00:44:00.950
our private information
retrieval protocol.

00:44:00.951 --> 00:44:02.550
But for the changed assumption,

00:44:02.551 --> 00:44:04.330
we show a new
lower bound saying,

00:44:04.331 --> 00:44:08.375
you cannot do anything
better even in this scenario.

00:44:08.376 --> 00:44:11.120
I've also worked
towards the notion

00:44:11.121 --> 00:44:12.975
of having an Oblivious
data structure.

00:44:12.976 --> 00:44:15.150
So, let us say,
an adversity already knows

00:44:15.151 --> 00:44:16.390
that you just have

00:44:16.391 --> 00:44:18.270
a map data structure or
a tree data structure.

00:44:18.271 --> 00:44:20.040
And in that case, trying to hide

00:44:20.041 --> 00:44:22.610
which operations are being
performed and done so,

00:44:22.611 --> 00:44:23.970
these Oblivious data structures

00:44:23.971 --> 00:44:26.215
can be faster than a neural.

00:44:26.216 --> 00:44:28.490
And finally, I'm presently

00:44:28.491 --> 00:44:30.390
looking towards
the notion of achieving

00:44:30.391 --> 00:44:33.220
locality in conjunction
with Obliviousness

00:44:33.221 --> 00:44:34.805
as well as a notion of achieving

00:44:34.806 --> 00:44:37.930
perfect security
with Obliviousness.

00:44:37.931 --> 00:44:41.000
In the area of Blockchains,

00:44:41.001 --> 00:44:41.945
I have worked towards

00:44:41.946 --> 00:44:43.460
adversarial behavior
in Blockchains,

00:44:43.461 --> 00:44:46.370
where we have tried to
attack the Bitcoin protocol,

00:44:46.371 --> 00:44:48.660
as well as I've worked
towards the design of

00:44:48.661 --> 00:44:49.950
a robust Blockchains both in

00:44:49.951 --> 00:44:53.395
the permissioned as well
as permissionless settings.

00:44:53.396 --> 00:44:56.585
With that, let me move onto

00:44:56.586 --> 00:44:59.280
speaking a little bit
about my future work.

00:44:59.281 --> 00:45:02.320
The overarching goal
over here is to move

00:45:02.321 --> 00:45:04.365
from privacy-preserving
computation

00:45:04.366 --> 00:45:06.200
to a privacy-preserving system.

00:45:06.201 --> 00:45:09.935
Until now, we have been
discussing having private inputs,

00:45:09.936 --> 00:45:11.935
performing some
computation on top of it,

00:45:11.936 --> 00:45:13.580
and learning some data models.

00:45:13.581 --> 00:45:16.200
For instance, we considered
Netflix ratings as being

00:45:16.201 --> 00:45:18.770
approved inputs and
user recommendations

00:45:18.771 --> 00:45:21.390
as being the output.

00:45:21.391 --> 00:45:23.635
But in an actual system,

00:45:23.636 --> 00:45:25.340
you may end up revealing

00:45:25.341 --> 00:45:28.150
this information through
other auxiliary means.

00:45:28.151 --> 00:45:31.555
For example, if it's
the Netflix's example,

00:45:31.556 --> 00:45:33.580
just the fact that you're
streaming movies from

00:45:33.581 --> 00:45:36.480
Netflix already tells that
I'm watching this movie.

00:45:36.481 --> 00:45:39.920
And that in practice
can hurt you.

00:45:39.921 --> 00:45:42.790
Second, the data model itself in

00:45:42.791 --> 00:45:44.170
some cases can reveal a lot of

00:45:44.171 --> 00:45:46.245
information about
your private inputs.

00:45:46.246 --> 00:45:47.505
And if that happens,

00:45:47.506 --> 00:45:49.000
the entire privacy-preserving
computation

00:45:49.001 --> 00:45:51.085
you're doing in between
is rendered useless.

00:45:51.086 --> 00:45:53.365
So, you may want to use

00:45:53.366 --> 00:45:55.730
notions of differentiate
privacy in conjunction

00:45:55.731 --> 00:45:58.400
with secure computation
to ensure that you still

00:45:58.401 --> 00:46:02.385
have some realistic guarantees
on your private inputs.

00:46:02.386 --> 00:46:05.205
Second, you'd want to move on

00:46:05.206 --> 00:46:07.140
to a broader class
of functions such

00:46:07.141 --> 00:46:09.110
as being privacy-preserving
neural networks

00:46:09.111 --> 00:46:11.300
or privacy-preserving
genomic tasks,

00:46:11.301 --> 00:46:14.320
as also design functions
that are better suited

00:46:14.321 --> 00:46:15.590
for secure computation or

00:46:15.591 --> 00:46:18.390
that are faster than
secure computation.

00:46:18.391 --> 00:46:20.900
Looking at slightly long term,

00:46:20.901 --> 00:46:21.460
we would want to

00:46:21.461 --> 00:46:23.985
consider alternative
security definitions.

00:46:23.986 --> 00:46:26.275
One such example
over here would be,

00:46:26.276 --> 00:46:28.010
for Obliviousness, right now,

00:46:28.011 --> 00:46:30.675
we are thinking of trying
to hide all access patterns.

00:46:30.676 --> 00:46:32.190
But what if we were to think of

00:46:32.191 --> 00:46:33.620
Obliviousness as
being a spectrum

00:46:33.621 --> 00:46:35.520
between leaking
all access patterns

00:46:35.521 --> 00:46:37.155
and hiding all access patterns.

00:46:37.156 --> 00:46:39.130
And now, we can try
to pick a point in

00:46:39.131 --> 00:46:42.200
between in a way that
you leak some accesses

00:46:42.201 --> 00:46:43.780
in such a way that you achieve

00:46:43.781 --> 00:46:48.745
some security in
some realistic notion.

00:46:48.746 --> 00:46:52.930
The eventual goal over here
is to ensure that a user or

00:46:52.931 --> 00:46:54.830
an organization
can specify policy

00:46:54.831 --> 00:46:57.265
on how data or
program can be used.

00:46:57.266 --> 00:47:00.070
And if all such users
have such a policy,

00:47:00.071 --> 00:47:01.985
a company who's
trying to provide

00:47:01.986 --> 00:47:05.255
user-specific features actually
adheres to this policy,

00:47:05.256 --> 00:47:09.670
and the privacy-preserving
system actually ensures this.

00:47:09.671 --> 00:47:13.180
In this space of
robust Blockchains,

00:47:13.181 --> 00:47:16.110
I'm interested in
combining the merits of

00:47:16.111 --> 00:47:17.845
the classical
consensus protocols

00:47:17.846 --> 00:47:19.665
and the new
permissionless protocols,

00:47:19.666 --> 00:47:21.545
as well as trying to
achieve the notion of

00:47:21.546 --> 00:47:25.345
confidentiality and efficiency
together in Blockchains.

00:47:25.346 --> 00:47:28.135
Before ending my talk,

00:47:28.136 --> 00:47:30.275
I would like to thank
all of my collaborators

00:47:30.276 --> 00:47:31.515
without whom none of

00:47:31.516 --> 00:47:33.345
my research would
have been possible.

00:47:33.346 --> 00:47:36.310
Thank you. And I'd be
happy to take questions.

00:47:36.311 --> 00:47:46.400
&gt;&gt; I've one question.

00:47:46.401 --> 00:47:49.400
You said that in
the Netflix experiment,

00:47:49.401 --> 00:47:52.155
it took several minutes,

00:47:52.156 --> 00:47:55.730
what if for
the unsecured scheme,

00:47:55.731 --> 00:47:58.175
how long will it
take in seconds?

00:47:58.176 --> 00:48:00.205
&gt;&gt; In case we
implement this now,

00:48:00.206 --> 00:48:02.330
for that particular example,

00:48:02.331 --> 00:48:04.625
the ratio of secure to

00:48:04.626 --> 00:48:07.190
insecure is 10 to the four
with secure computation.

00:48:07.191 --> 00:48:07.501
&gt;&gt; About 10?

00:48:07.501 --> 00:48:09.540
&gt;&gt; Ten to the four with
secure computation.

00:48:09.541 --> 00:48:10.330
&gt;&gt; Ten to the?

00:48:10.331 --> 00:48:12.160
&gt;&gt; Ten to the four. So,
what makes one minute,

00:48:12.161 --> 00:48:13.945
will take 10,000 minutes.

00:48:13.946 --> 00:48:17.465
And if you assume
a trusted processor,

00:48:17.466 --> 00:48:23.050
there has been vulnerable [inaudible]
and with the same algorithms,

00:48:23.051 --> 00:48:27.970
it is around a factor
of, I think 4,280.

00:48:30.170 --> 00:48:34.460
&gt;&gt; So, why do you
think a notion of

00:48:34.461 --> 00:48:38.190
non private security like something
[inaudible] is a good idea?

00:48:38.191 --> 00:48:41.730
&gt;&gt; Because you'll get
some notion of efficiency.

00:48:41.731 --> 00:48:43.280
You're talking about
Obliviousness spectrum?

00:48:43.281 --> 00:48:44.570
&gt;&gt; Right, right.

00:48:44.571 --> 00:48:46.820
&gt;&gt; So, If you can try
to reduce the number of

00:48:46.821 --> 00:48:49.800
accesses or the number
Oblivious accesses,

00:48:49.801 --> 00:48:52.370
you'll get some notion
of efficiency.

00:48:52.371 --> 00:48:55.385
&gt;&gt; Right. But wouldn't
it be very hard to

00:48:55.386 --> 00:48:57.390
define what secure your privacy

00:48:57.391 --> 00:48:59.090
really means in that case?

00:48:59.091 --> 00:49:01.090
&gt;&gt; That is something that
I'm presently working on,

00:49:01.091 --> 00:49:02.500
and trying to understand what

00:49:02.501 --> 00:49:04.230
security exactly
means in that context.

00:49:04.231 --> 00:49:06.300
You're right. It's
not so easy to define.

00:49:06.301 --> 00:49:09.240
So, one notion that can be

00:49:09.241 --> 00:49:10.620
interesting is the notion

00:49:10.621 --> 00:49:12.530
of traced differentially
private computation,

00:49:12.531 --> 00:49:14.960
so that you reveal
some accesses in such a way

00:49:14.961 --> 00:49:18.230
that the differential privacy

00:49:18.231 --> 00:49:19.780
of users is still maintained.

00:49:19.781 --> 00:49:22.800
And that may not be
applicable for all inputs.

00:49:22.801 --> 00:49:24.950
So, instead of thinking of

00:49:24.951 --> 00:49:27.340
Obliviousness as being
the worst case scenario.

00:49:27.341 --> 00:49:29.985
Therefore, any input I'm
going to give you a solution.

00:49:29.986 --> 00:49:31.440
If you have let's say, maybe a

00:49:31.441 --> 00:49:33.505
distribution on input or
something along those lines,

00:49:33.506 --> 00:49:36.315
you're trying to hide
something specific.

00:49:36.316 --> 00:49:38.530
Again, I'm not being
very specific here

00:49:38.531 --> 00:49:39.590
because this is not
something that I

00:49:39.591 --> 00:49:40.905
have thought about entirely,

00:49:40.906 --> 00:49:44.620
but this notion seems like
a reasonable one that we

00:49:44.621 --> 00:49:48.250
can try to work on.

00:49:48.251 --> 00:49:49.505
&gt;&gt; I think that
that's kind is a,

00:49:49.506 --> 00:49:51.620
for the approval of obfuscation,

00:49:51.621 --> 00:49:55.330
why does it require
Oblivious RAM?

00:49:55.331 --> 00:49:59.370
Because this approval
was encrypted even though

00:49:59.371 --> 00:50:01.550
the access it's really hard

00:50:01.551 --> 00:50:04.295
to reverse engineer
the original program?

00:50:04.296 --> 00:50:09.105
&gt;&gt; So, I think a simple example
would be if I have

00:50:09.106 --> 00:50:11.860
a program which performs
binary search versus

00:50:11.861 --> 00:50:14.935
a program that is adding
elements in an array,

00:50:14.936 --> 00:50:16.560
just based on access patterns,

00:50:16.561 --> 00:50:18.815
you'll not know exactly
this is what it is doing,

00:50:18.816 --> 00:50:20.570
but perhaps you
should be able to

00:50:20.571 --> 00:50:21.980
figure out at some point

00:50:21.981 --> 00:50:23.895
in time that is
what it is doing.

00:50:23.896 --> 00:50:30.770
&gt;&gt; That's only applies to
a very simple program, right?

00:50:30.771 --> 00:50:32.250
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:50:32.251 --> 00:50:34.690
I think what we're
trying to get is to able

00:50:34.691 --> 00:50:38.700
guarantees with having
a cryptographic proof.

00:50:38.701 --> 00:50:40.175
So, you're right.

00:50:40.176 --> 00:50:41.510
Even the generic case,

00:50:41.511 --> 00:50:43.825
it will be very hard to
actually construct an attack.

00:50:43.826 --> 00:50:46.025
But over here, what you're
trying to show is that,

00:50:46.026 --> 00:50:47.575
if you have something like this,

00:50:47.576 --> 00:50:49.170
you will never be
able to reconstruct

00:50:49.171 --> 00:50:50.380
or come up with an attack.

00:50:50.381 --> 00:50:53.550
&gt;&gt; Right. There are
real world cases that

00:50:53.551 --> 00:50:56.930
people care about
the program obfuscation, right?

00:50:56.931 --> 00:51:01.080
And in those real world cases,

00:51:01.081 --> 00:51:04.375
is Oblivious RAM unnecessary?

00:51:04.376 --> 00:51:08.850
&gt;&gt; So, I would say if
in real world cases,

00:51:08.851 --> 00:51:11.950
if they cared about
these settings,

00:51:11.951 --> 00:51:14.445
then you would want to
actually hide the program.

00:51:14.446 --> 00:51:17.615
And in that case, yes, Oblivious
RAM would be necessary.

00:51:17.616 --> 00:51:19.030
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:51:19.031 --> 00:51:21.380
&gt;&gt; So, if you're doing,
never mind. Sorry.

00:51:21.381 --> 00:51:24.905
&gt;&gt; Well, it's okay.
I've got you there.

00:51:24.906 --> 00:51:27.290
&gt;&gt; Did you have a question?

00:51:27.291 --> 00:51:29.490
&gt;&gt; No.

00:51:29.491 --> 00:51:33.180
But I can't take here.

